==============================================================================
 PHASE 0 NOTES - THE JAVA BRIDGE  (written in simple language)
==============================================================================

Goal of Phase 0:
  Get comfortable with Java and Maven BEFORE building the real analyzer.
  We built a tiny program that holds facts about some files, groups them
  by package, and prints a neat sorted list. Nothing is analyzed for real
  yet. The numbers are typed in by hand.

Think of Phase 0 as learning to drive in an empty parking lot before
driving on the road.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PART 1 - HOW JAVA CODE BECOMES A RUNNING PROGRAM
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Three steps. Remember them as WRITE -> TRANSLATE -> RUN.

  1. WRITE     You write FileMetrics.java. This is just text for humans.

  2. TRANSLATE javac (the Java compiler) reads your text and produces
               FileMetrics.class. That file holds "bytecode".
               Bytecode is a simple instruction language that no real CPU
               understands directly, but every JVM does.

  3. RUN       The JVM (Java Virtual Machine) reads the .class file and
               executes it on your actual computer.

Everyday picture:
  .java     = a recipe written in English
  javac     = a translator turning it into a universal kitchen code
  .class    = the recipe in universal kitchen code
  JVM       = a kitchen robot that can cook any recipe in that code,
              whether the kitchen is Mac, Windows, or Linux

This is why Java says "write once, run anywhere". The same .class file
runs on any computer that has a JVM.

Words you will hear:
  JDK = Java Development Kit = JVM + javac + other tools. Developers need it.
  JRE = Java Runtime Environment = only the JVM + libraries. Enough to RUN.
  JVM = the robot that actually runs bytecode.

Where you can SEE this in our project:
  backend/src/main/java/.../FileMetrics.java       <- your text  (step 1)
  backend/target/classes/.../FileMetrics.class     <- bytecode   (step 2)
  java -cp target/classes dev.codepulse.Phase0Main <- run it     (step 3)


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PART 2 - MAVEN: THE BUILD ASSISTANT
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

What problem does Maven solve?
  Without Maven you would type javac commands by hand for every file,
  download JUnit yourself, and remember the right order of steps.
  Maven does all of that from one file: pom.xml.

If you know npm:  pom.xml          is like package.json
                  ~/.m2/repository is like node_modules (but shared)
                  ./mvnw test      is like npm test

Maven works in fixed STEPS called "lifecycle phases". Asking for a step
runs every step before it:

  validate      -> is pom.xml okay?
  compile       -> turn src/main/java into bytecode   (target/classes)
  test-compile  -> turn src/test/java into bytecode   (target/test-classes)
  test          -> run the tests
  package       -> build a .jar file (not needed yet)

So when you type  ./mvnw test  Maven ALSO compiles first.

What is mvnw? (the "Maven Wrapper")
  A small script saved in the project. It downloads the EXACT Maven
  version written in .mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties (3.9.16).
  Your laptop, a friend's laptop, and a CI server all build with the
  same Maven. No "works on my machine" problems.

What is in our pom.xml?
  - Java version 21
  - ONE dependency: JUnit, with "test scope" = used only for tests,
    never shipped inside the final program
  - Two plugins: compiler (does "compile") and surefire (does "test")


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PART 3 - CLASS, OBJECT, INTERFACE, RECORD
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  CLASS     = a blueprint. "A house has rooms and a door."
  OBJECT    = one real thing built from the blueprint. "My house."
              You make one with  new  :  new PackageSummary(list)
  INTERFACE = a promise of what something can DO, with no data.
              "Anything that is a Vehicle can drive()."
              (We will use one in Phase 1 for reading source files.)
  RECORD    = a short special class made only to CARRY DATA that
              never changes.

Our record, in one line:

  public record FileMetrics(String relativePath, String packageName,
                            int physicalLoc, int methodCount) { }

From that one line Java writes FOR you:
  - a constructor:  new FileMetrics("A.java", "p", 10, 1)
  - getters:        m.relativePath()   m.physicalLoc()   ...
                    (note: NOT getRelativePath())
  - equals()        two records with the same 4 values are "equal"
  - hashCode()      a number made from the 4 values (see Part 6)
  - toString()      nice printing: FileMetrics[relativePath=A.java, ...]

All fields are final: once created, a FileMetrics can never change.

The "compact constructor" inside FileMetrics is a guard at the door.
It runs BEFORE the object is created and throws an error if the data is
bad (null path, negative line count). A broken FileMetrics can never
exist.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PART 4 - GENERICS: THE <...> BRACKETS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The angle brackets say WHAT TYPE goes inside a collection.

  List<FileMetrics>          a list that can only hold FileMetrics
  Set<String>                a set that can only hold Strings
  Map<String, Set<String>>   a map where
                               key   = String       (package name)
                               value = Set<String>  (file paths)

Read Map<String, Set<String>> as:
  "a map FROM a package name TO a set of file paths".

Example of what it holds:
  "dev.codepulse"        -> { "Phase0Main.java" }
  "dev.codepulse.engine" -> { "FileMetrics.java", "PackageSummary.java" }

Why bother? If you try to put a number into a List<FileMetrics>, the
compiler refuses BEFORE the program runs. Errors caught early are cheap.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PART 5 - SORTED AND "SAME EVERY TIME" (DETERMINISTIC)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  HashMap / HashSet  = fast, but the order you get back is unpredictable.
  TreeMap / TreeSet  = always keeps things in sorted (A to Z) order.

CodePulse must print the SAME report for the SAME code every time.
So PackageSummary uses TreeMap and TreeSet. That is why the output is
always alphabetical, whatever order we put the files in.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PART 6 - EQUALS, HASHCODE, AND THE "LOST KEY" PROBLEM  (gate question)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Two ways to compare objects:

  a == b         "Are these the SAME object in memory?"  (same house?)
  a.equals(b)    "Do these have the SAME content?"       (same design?)

Always use .equals() for Strings and records. Using == is a classic bug.

How a HashSet finds things - a locker room picture:
  - hashCode() is a number that decides WHICH LOCKER the object goes in.
  - equals() checks the objects INSIDE that locker to find the match.

  Adding "A.java":  hashCode says locker 7  -> stored in locker 7.
  Looking it up:    hashCode says locker 7  -> open 7 -> found it!

Now the danger. Suppose the object can change (it is "mutable"):

  1. Add key with path "A.java"     -> goes into locker 7.
  2. Change its path to "B.java"    -> object is STILL sitting in locker 7.
  3. Ask: does the set contain it?  hashCode("B.java") says locker 3.
                                    Open locker 3 -> empty -> "NO".
  4. Try to remove it               -> also looks in locker 3 -> fails.
  5. set.size() still says 1        -> it is there, but lost forever.

That is exactly what CollectionEqualityTest proves.

Lesson: anything you put in a Set, or use as a Map key, must NOT change.
That is why FileMetrics is a record, and why in Phase 3 the import-graph
edges will also be records.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PART 7 - DEFENSIVE COPY
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Problem: someone hands you a list, keeps a reference to it, and changes
it later. Your object's data changes behind your back.

Fix: make your OWN copy when you receive it.

  this.metrics = List.copyOf(metrics);

Picture: a friend lends you their notebook. Instead of keeping THEIR
notebook (which they may scribble in later), you photocopy it and keep
the photocopy. List.copyOf also makes the copy read-only.

Test that proves it:
  PackageSummaryTest.isNotAffectedByLaterChangesToTheInputList


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PART 8 - EXCEPTIONS AND READING A STACK TRACE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

An exception is Java's way of shouting "something went wrong, I cannot
continue". If nobody catches it, the program stops and prints a
STACK TRACE.

Run:  java -cp target/classes dev.codepulse.Phase0Main --duplicate

You get:

  Exception in thread "main" java.lang.IllegalArgumentException:
        duplicate relativePath: src/main/java/App.java
    at ...PackageSummary.rejectDuplicatePaths(PackageSummary.java:37)
    at ...PackageSummary.<init>(PackageSummary.java:29)
    at ...Phase0Main.failWithDuplicatePath(Phase0Main.java:42)
    at ...Phase0Main.main(Phase0Main.java:16)

How to read it:
  First line    WHAT went wrong (type + message).
  "at" lines    WHERE, newest first. A trail of footprints:
                main (line 16) called failWithDuplicatePath (line 42),
                which called the constructor <init> (line 29),
                which called rejectDuplicatePaths (line 37) <- failed here.
  <init>        is Java's internal name for a constructor.

Tip: find the TOP "at" line that is in YOUR code, open that line number.

Two kinds of exceptions:
  Checked   (e.g. IOException) - Java FORCES you to handle or declare it.
            For things outside your control: file missing, network down.
  Unchecked (e.g. IllegalArgumentException) - not forced.
            For programming mistakes, like passing a duplicate path.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PART 9 - THE DEBUGGER (A PAUSE BUTTON FOR YOUR PROGRAM)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A breakpoint pauses the program on a line so you can look inside the
variables, then move forward one line at a time.

VS Code steps:
  1. Open backend/src/main/java/dev/codepulse/engine/PackageSummary.java
  2. Click just LEFT of the line number of
         if (!seen.add(m.relativePath())) {
     A red dot appears. That is your breakpoint.
  3. Open backend/src/main/java/dev/codepulse/Phase0Main.java
  4. The program needs the word --duplicate. Do this once:
       Run menu -> Add Configuration... -> choose "Java".
       In the launch.json that opens, find the entry for Phase0Main
       and add:   "args": "--duplicate"
  5. Press F5 (Run -> Start Debugging) and pick that configuration.
  6. The program pauses on the red dot. Look at the left side panel:
       VARIABLES  -> expand "seen" (empty the first time) and "m".
       CALL STACK -> the same 4 footprints as the stack trace.
  7. Press F5 to continue to the next pause. On the SECOND pause,
     "seen" already contains "src/main/java/App.java" and "m" has the
     same path. seen.add(...) returns false, the if is true, and the
     throw runs. Press F10 (Step Over) to watch it happen line by line.

Command-line version (jdb, comes with the JDK):
  cd backend
  jdb -classpath target/classes -sourcepath src/main/java dev.codepulse.Phase0Main --duplicate
  > stop in dev.codepulse.engine.PackageSummary.rejectDuplicatePaths
  > run
  > locals          (show variables)
  > next            (move one line forward; repeat)
  > print seen
  > cont            (continue)
  > quit


==============================================================================
 HOW TO TEST EVERYTHING, STEP BY STEP  (and what each output means)
==============================================================================

STEP 1 - Open a terminal in the backend folder
  cd ~/Desktop/CodePulse/backend

STEP 2 - Run all tests
  ./mvnw test

  What you should see near the end:

    Running dev.codepulse.engine.CollectionEqualityTest
    Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0
    Running dev.codepulse.engine.PackageSummaryTest
    Tests run: 4, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0
    Tests run: 7, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0
    BUILD SUCCESS

  What it means:
    Tests run: 7   -> 7 small checks ran.
    Failures: 0    -> no check found a wrong answer.
    Errors: 0      -> no check crashed unexpectedly.
    BUILD SUCCESS  -> compiled fine AND all tests passed.

  If you see BUILD FAILURE: scroll UP to the first line starting with
  [ERROR]. It names the test and shows expected vs actual values.

  What each test proves:
    PackageSummaryTest
      groupsFilesByPackageInSortedOrder -> grouping + A-to-Z sorting works
      emptyInputProducesEmptySummary    -> no files = empty output, no crash
      rejectsDuplicateRelativePaths     -> same path twice = error
      isNotAffectedByLaterChanges...    -> defensive copy works (Part 7)
    CollectionEqualityTest
      recordsWithSameValuesAreEqual...  -> records compare by content
      aClassWithoutEquals...            -> plain class compares by identity
      mutatingAKeyAfterInsertion...     -> the "lost key" problem (Part 6)

STEP 3 - Run the program normally
  java -cp target/classes dev.codepulse.Phase0Main

  Output:
    (default package) (1 files)
      Scratch.java  loc=8 methods=1
    dev.codepulse (1 files)
      src/main/java/dev/codepulse/Phase0Main.java  loc=25 methods=1
    dev.codepulse.engine (2 files)
      src/main/java/dev/codepulse/engine/FileMetrics.java  loc=40 methods=0
      src/main/java/dev/codepulse/engine/PackageSummary.java  loc=95 methods=5

  What it means:
    Each block is one Java package, followed by the files inside it.
    loc     = number of lines in that file   (typed by hand for now)
    methods = number of methods in that file (typed by hand for now)
    "(default package)" = a file with no "package" line at the top.

  Why it is useful:
    In Phase0Main the files are listed OUT of order on purpose, yet the
    output is sorted. That proves the report is deterministic. This is
    a tiny preview of the real CodePulse report: later phases will fill
    these numbers by actually reading Java source code.

STEP 4 - Run the deliberate crash
  java -cp target/classes dev.codepulse.Phase0Main --duplicate

  Output: the stack trace shown in Part 8.
  What it means: our safety check found two files with the same path
  and refused to continue. That is correct behavior, not a bug.
  Why it is useful: practice reading stack traces, which you will do
  many times in this project.

STEP 5 - Run tests inside VS Code (optional, nicer view)
  Open PackageSummaryTest.java. Click the green triangle next to the
  class name or next to any @Test method. Green tick = passed,
  red cross = failed. The "Testing" panel (flask icon on the left bar)
  lists all 7 tests.

STEP 6 - Break a test on purpose (the best way to learn)
  In PackageSummaryTest, change
      List.of("dev.codepulse", "dev.codepulse.engine")
  to  List.of("dev.codepulse.engine", "dev.codepulse")
  Run ./mvnw test. You get BUILD FAILURE with something like
      expected: <[dev.codepulse.engine, dev.codepulse]>
      but was:  <[dev.codepulse, dev.codepulse.engine]>
  Read it, change it back, and confirm BUILD SUCCESS again.


